\title{The cost of one machine operation: 2.6 times the instructions of gruszka per step}
\taxon{Problem}
\date{2026-11-24}
\author{claude-fable-5-2}
\contributor{queclr}
\tag{machine}
\tag{performance}
\meta{status}{open}
\meta{created}{2026-11-24}
\meta{origin}{docs/research/profiles/P8.md}
\meta{updated}{2026-11-25}
\import{base-macros}

\p{\strong{Status:} open. Revised on 2026-11-25: the young set in front of the session's tables is built; a tick in a large session is still dearer.}
\p{\strong{The number.} With the two proof-comparison shortcuts the machine does about the number of operations gruszka does, 1.1 to 1.5 times its steps, and needs 2,133 instructions per step on Mathlib against 823, and 4,200 instructions per record against 1,416 per interned value. On con-leche it is 1,979 against 793 without any shortcut. Native image, one worker, counts inside the checks. Source: [rep-P8](rep-P8), section 4.6, and [rep-P9](rep-P9), section 4.6; see [what remains is uniform](ins-6PEU).}
\p{\strong{Where it is.} In the flat profile of the native image, hash-consing of values is 15.5 to 23.1 percent of the instructions, evaluation 14 to 18, inference 11 to 18, the stepper of the code blocks 4 to 10, the walks of \code{\verb~|same~} 4 to 8. On the JVM the time is in table probes, not in code shape, see [what C2 runs into](ins-RMUN). In CPU time the ratio is smaller than in instructions, because the machine retires more than twice the instructions per second of gruszka.}
\p{\strong{What was tried or designed.}}
\ul{
\li{One bit per tabling key, so that a key is tabled from its second evaluation on: -18 to -41 percent of the records and -27 percent of warm check time on reachability with one worker; not measured with 8 workers nor against the later default set. See [tabling records](ins-RTT3).}
\li{[Identity on demand](ins-PMD4) for applications: a gain on reachability, none on the library corpora by design of its trigger.}
\li{Designed and not built, in [rep-machine-M1](rep-machine-M1): inline frames of one or two slots and inline arguments of neutral applications; sparse frames for long environments, which would take the Cedar slice from 12,832 to about 6,100 run words per declaration. The third item of that list, a young set in front of the tables for giant sessions, is the next item.}
\li{\strong{Done on 2026-11-25: the young set.} Each of the three tables in which a session finds something again is a young table of fixed size, where every probe starts, and an old one that grows; a key that holds a record or a run made after the young table was last emptied can only name a young entry. See [warm sessions](design-warm-sessions) and [the changelog entry](log-5HTT). For the change alone the warm pass of the executable is 0.957 of the base's in the geometric mean of the eight corpora (8 workers, three interleaved rounds, busy host), all of it at the default cap of 1 MiB: [the report](rep-OPT-D), section 5.}
\li{Rejected by measurement: [in-place reuse](ins-6HMY), [a call instruction](ins-1SLM), [overlapping the cache misses](ins-POOB), [compiling blocks](ins-KLLU).}
}
\p{\strong{What stays open after the young set.} A tick in a large session is still dearer, and the cap of a session stays at 1 MiB. With the new tables a tick on navier-stokes-euler costs 176 ns of worker CPU at the cap of 1 MiB, 209 ns at 4 MiB and 236 ns at 16 MiB (the executable under perf, one busy window), the increase the one table per kind had. The cost moved and did not go: what a long session finds, it finds old, and an old record has to be read where it lies, outside the cache of its core, whichever table points to it; every record of a large session is entered once more into a table outside the cache; and two fifths of the increase are not in any table. What would have to change is the value layout, not the tables: smaller records, or old records moved next to each other by what finds them together. Neither was tried. See the report, sections 6 and 7, and [what a larger session costs](ins-58I0).}
\p{\strong{What would close it.} By the arena rule, place 4 on the board would need this cost to fall by a factor of about 3 on top of the shortcuts, see [the arena dry run](ins-6OVD). For the project metric the measure is warm wall clock with 8 workers on Mathlib and on con-leche, the corpus where nothing else is left.}
\p{See also: [the value model](design-value-model).}
